---
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import SecretGenerator from '../../components/SecretGenerator.jsx';
---
<ToolLayout title="Kubernetes Secret Template Generator" description="Generate a Secret YAML template with base64-encoded values, for local development use.">
  <SecretGenerator client:load slot="tool" />

  <h2>Why base64 in Secrets isn't the same as encryption</h2>
  <p>Kubernetes Secrets store values base64-encoded, not encrypted — anyone with API access to view the Secret can trivially decode it. Base64 exists for safe transport of arbitrary data in YAML/JSON, not for confidentiality, which is a common and important misconception.</p>

  <h2>How to use this generator</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Enter your key-value pairs.</li>
    <li>Copy the generated Secret YAML with base64-encoded values.</li>
    <li>Apply with <code>kubectl apply -f secret.yaml</code>.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Common Kubernetes Secret Mistakes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming base64 encoding provides security</strong> — it's trivially reversible; real protection comes from Kubernetes RBAC controlling who can read Secrets, and optionally encryption at rest.</li>
    <li><strong>Committing generated Secret YAML files to version control</strong>, exposing the encoded (easily decoded) values in your repo history.</li>
    <li><strong>Using this approach for production secrets</strong> without additional tooling — production setups often use a secrets manager (Vault, cloud KMS) rather than plain Kubernetes Secrets.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>When to Use This Secret Generator</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Quickly generating a Secret manifest for local development or testing</li>
    <li>Learning the Secret YAML structure and base64 encoding requirement</li>
    <li>Bootstrapping a Secret template before integrating with a proper secrets management tool</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Kubernetes Secret FAQ</h2>
  <p><strong>Is this safe for production secrets?</strong> Not on its own — for production, use Kubernetes RBAC to restrict access, consider encryption at rest, and evaluate a dedicated secrets manager for sensitive production credentials.</p>
</ToolLayout>
